---
import { buildMounts, defaultLoadout } from "@void-drift/mode-a";
import { ENGINE_ART } from "../../../../../../packages/mode-a/src/lib/engine-art";
import { FIGHTER } from "../../../../../../packages/mode-a/src/lib/fighter";
import { slotAccepts } from "../../../../../../packages/mode-a/src/lib/schemas/loadout";
import { WASP } from "../../../../../../packages/mode-a/src/lib/wasp";

const fitMounts = (["wasp", "fighter"] as const).map((ship) =>
  buildMounts(defaultLoadout(ship))
    .filter((m) => m.group === "hull")
    .slice(0, 1)
    .map(({ x, y }) => ({ x, y })),
);
const fitEngines = (["wasp", "fighter"] as const).map((ship) =>
  buildMounts(defaultLoadout(ship))
    .filter((m) => slotAccepts(m.type, "E"))
    .map((m) => ({
      x: m.x,
      y: m.y,
      angle: m.angle,
      below: ship !== "wasp" && m.group === "side",
      faces: [
        ...ENGINE_ART.ion[
          ship === "wasp" ? 3 : m.key === "left" ? 1 : m.key === "right" ? 2 : 3
        ],
      ],
    })),
);
const fitFighter: { points: readonly (readonly number[])[]; color: string }[] =
  FIGHTER.hull.map((p, i) => {
    const next = FIGHTER.hull[(i + 1) % FIGHTER.hull.length];
    return {
      points: [[-3, 0], p, next],
      color: FIGHTER.facets[(p[1] + next[1]) / 2 < 0 ? i % 3 : 3 + (i % 3)],
    };
  });
fitFighter.push(
  { points: [...FIGHTER.ridge], color: "#a2d8df" },
  {
    points: [
      [38, 0],
      [-21, 0],
      [-15, 4],
    ],
    color: "#51869e",
  },
  { points: [...FIGHTER.cockpit], color: "#000" },
);

import GravityCoilAssembly from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilAssembly.astro";
import GravityCoilBelonging from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilBelonging.astro";
import GravityCoilConstruction from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilConstruction.astro";
import GravityCoilDensity from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilDensity.astro";
import GravityCoilDepth from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilDepth.astro";
import GravityCoilFit from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilFit.svelte";
import GravityCoilForms from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilForms.astro";
import GravityCoilGraphicMachines from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilGraphicMachines.astro";
import GravityCoilLight from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilLight.astro";
import GravityCoilMachines from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilMachines.astro";
import GravityCoilNine from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilNine.astro";
import GravityCoilPlanes from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilPlanes.astro";
import GravityCoilReferences from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilReferences.astro";
import GravityCoilSeating from "../../../components/studies/GravityCoilSeating.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "gravity-coil-2026-11-03");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <p>
    This study is stopped and preserved as reference. Continue in
    <a href="/lab/studies/gravity-coil-form-2026-11-03"
      >Gravity coil: static visual form</a
    >.
  </p>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A winding with weight</h2>
    <p>
      A Gravity coil mounted beside an engine should look like a deliberately
      built piece of technical machinery before any effect appears. A toroid,
      coil or spring supplies its physical identity: repeated solid windings,
      space between them, and a convincing structure holding them to the ship.
      The first design question is which construction gives that identity weight
      and clarity in the game’s top-down view.
    </p>
    <p>
      Imagine the fitted ship outside a gravity field, with the coil inactive.
      Its opening and winding must still distinguish it from an engine or an
      energy store. Enlarged, the assembly should reveal purposeful parts; at
      flight scale, its principal masses and gaps must carry the same idea. Flat
      facets, light from above-left and restrained material contrast connect it
      to the existing support modules. Technical character must come from
      construction, rather than fine lines that disappear when the ship shrinks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The coil’s physical parts remain static, including when active. Like an
      engine, it produces separate FX: field interaction or exotic physics on
      the FX layer, with no moving or animated mechanical parts. This study
      begins with the physical object. After its appearance, the next visual
      question is how its active FX within a gravity field interact with engine
      effects to create interesting combined visuals. Effect shape, rhythm and
      overlap remain to be explored; charging rules and flight balance are
      outside this initial inquiry.
    </p>
    <GravityCoilReferences />
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Construction before effects</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>02.1 / What space does the winding enclose? — findings</summary>
      <p>
        A closed toroid concentrates the machine around a single aperture. An
        axial spring spreads its winding along a direction. These static
        sketches compare that structural choice using the same cradle, materials
        and approximate envelope. Broad lit faces and dark side faces describe
        solid parts.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilForms />
      <p>
        Finding: these examples do not communicate in the game’s visual
        language, so they cannot settle the toroid-versus-spring question. This
        was a useful learning step, not a construction decision or an error to
        delete.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A support component must have no base or background plate. Such a
          plate fails beneath a ship and makes the object look like an isometric
          icon.
        </li>
        <li>
          Too much detail makes the component messy at 1×. Small comparisons
          must use 1×; the original 0.65× presentation was incorrect and is
          corrected here.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An exploration step can support a decision, teach something, or be an
        error that can be deleted. These drawings remain as evidence of the
        finding; neither construction has been selected.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="density">
        02.2 / How few sections can carry the coil? — B preferred
      </summary>
      <p>
        A bare ring with four broad winding sections is compared with eight
        narrower sections. Both have the same aperture, outside dimensions, two
        attachment tabs and material. There is no backing surface: the void is
        visible through the centre and between sections. The view is directly
        from above, with bevels describing thickness.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilDensity />
      <p>
        This tests how repetition and broad surfaces read at 1× before adding
        technical detail. The toroid is a controlled fixture for this question,
        not a selection over the spring. The 36 px width is provisional; fitted
        clearance and under-hull occlusion remain untested. All parts are
        static, and no FX are shown.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Review finding:</strong>
        B, the eight-section ring, is clearly preferred over A. Its distinct
        parts are worth carrying forward. This establishes a promising
        reference, while spring and helix constructions remain open. The
        comparison above preserves the reviewed grey supports.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="forms">
        02.3 / Nine constructions from the segmented ring
      </summary>
      <p>
        The reference below retains B’s geometry and restores yellow connectors.
        All nine descriptions carry forward distinct substantial parts, open
        gaps, no backing plate and static construction. They explore form before
        FX. For longitudinal forms, the coil axis runs fore–aft along the ship:
        the game’s overhead camera sees the side of the spring.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilDensity selected />
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Segmented toroid.</strong>
          Keep B’s eight faceted sectors around one open centre, gripped by two
          short yellow side connectors. The existing ring is the reference for
          compactness and readable separation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Elongated toroid.</strong>
          Stretch that ring along the ship’s fore–aft axis into an oval,
          retaining eight sections and yellow connectors at its waist. This
          tests a longer silhouette without losing the single aperture.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Split horseshoe.</strong>
          Open the aft end of the segmented ring, ending in two thick yellow
          terminal blocks. The central void opens into space, giving the
          circular machine a clear direction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Twin rings.</strong>
          Place two smaller segmented toroids fore and aft, joined by a short
          yellow bridge. Two open centres and a narrow waist create a multipart
          silhouette; resemblance to the Potential Well would need particular
          scrutiny.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Three-turn barrel spring.</strong>
          Lay one thick helical band along the fore–aft axis between yellow end
          collars. Three broad turns and generous gaps expose the winding from
          the side; the returning band falls into darker facets behind the upper
          portions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Segmented helix.</strong>
          Build that longitudinal winding from six chunky curved pieces with
          short recessed joints. Yellow end clamps anchor the assembly. The
          stepped progression carries B’s separate-part character into a
          continuous spiral.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tapered spring.</strong>
          Use three heavy turns that decrease in diameter toward the aft
          terminal. Yellow collars cap the broad forward end and narrow aft end.
          The taper supplies direction, with a risk of reading as an engine
          nozzle.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paired counter-wound springs.</strong>
          Place two short, thick helices beside each other, both running
          fore–aft but winding in opposite directions. Separate yellow end
          clamps leave a dark channel between them. This tests paired machinery
          without a shared background plate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Spaced induction hoops.</strong>
          Arrange three separate thick hoops perpendicular to the fore–aft axis,
          held by two narrow yellow side connectors. Viewed from above, their
          raised crowns and dark inner returns form a short barrel. This tests
          whether discrete hoops communicate the coil more clearly than a
          continuous helix at 1×.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        These are written construction proposals, not selected designs. The next
        drawings can compare a retained ring against a longitudinal
        construction; continuity, depth and readability at 1× still need visual
        evidence.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="nine">
        02.4 / Nine forms — toroid, horseshoe and spring retained
      </summary>
      <p>
        The nine written constructions, in the same order. Yellow parts connect
        or support the winding; none has a background plate. Fore is up and aft
        is down. The longitudinal springs are viewed from above the ship,
        showing the side of their horizontal axes. All examples remain static.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilNine />
      <p>
        Each small sample uses one artwork unit per CSS pixel (1×). Enlarged
        views expose construction; dimensions remain provisional. The ring
        reference is retained as 01. Compare how the other forms carry its
        separate-part character, especially the broad turns of 05 and the
        interrupted winding of 06. These are form studies, not fitted modules or
        FX previews.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained forms:</strong>
        01 Segmented toroid, 03 Split horseshoe and 05 Three-turn barrel spring.
        The finding is that simple, effective shapes work at both 1× and 8×.
        These originals remain the references for subsequent construction.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="machines">
        02.5 / Supports that complete the machine — findings
      </summary>
      <p>
        Three new arrangements for each retained form: toroid across the first
        row, horseshoe across the second, spring across the third. The
        white–charcoal working parts stay unchanged. Yellow forks, inner
        structures and open braces explore how the object holds together when
        some of it projects over space or sits on a chassis. No arrangement has
        a base or background plate.
      </p>

      <GravityCoilMachines />
      <p>
        <strong>02.5 findings:</strong>
        Free, underwing and on-chassis views expose different failures
        immediately. A free-standing module must connect its own parts; the hull
        cannot supply missing structure. The external toroid brace (3),
        horseshoe side struts (6) and spring arrangements leave unsupported or
        floating-looking pieces. The inner bearing ring (2) adds too much small
        detail and too many gaps. Yellow lines alone read as schematic marks
        rather than solid machinery.
      </p>
      <p>
        All three spring proposals remain unconvincing as enlarged parts-list
        objects, even where their 1× silhouettes suggest useful ideas. Supports
        and connectors need their own volume and the same upper-left lighting as
        the working parts. No support arrangement from 02.5 is selected.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="solid-machines">
        02.6 / Convincing graphic form — findings
      </summary>
      <p>
        The first 02.6 attempt confirmed the earlier finding: isometric-looking,
        decorative icon art is far from this game’s visual language. Its raised
        blocks and offset extrusions made the equipment bulky and literal. Those
        drawings have been removed. The direction is sleek, streamlined science
        fiction, expressed through restrained graphic forms.
      </p>
      <p>
        The equipment needs to look convincing, not physically realistic.
        Connections need to read as deliberate paths between parts; they do not
        need to explain a mechanically complete assembly. A few chosen planes
        can suggest volume and character without modelling a real material or
        object. The feeling of connection, weight and belonging to the ship is
        the criterion. Literal metal, plastic, bevels or structural detail are
        not requirements.
      </p>
      <p>
        The retained toroid, horseshoe and spring provide the working shapes.
        The next visual exploration should add only enough graphic structure to
        make their relationships convincing, preserving the clarity of their
        silhouettes and open spaces. Upper-left light remains a shared cue for
        volume, not a reason to introduce an isometric viewpoint. Free,
        underwing and on-chassis views remain the comparison; the replacement
        drawings follow in 02.7.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="graphic-machines">
        02.7 / Connection through graphic form — selections
      </summary>
      <p>
        The retained ring and horseshoe treatments. Narrow ribbons, swept paths
        and quiet charcoal returns suggest continuity without raised blocks or
        offset extrusions. The working shapes remain the reference: the added
        structure should bring them together without becoming the main subject.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilGraphicMachines />
      <p>
        The question is whether each drawing feels like one purposeful piece of
        ship equipment. The paths are aesthetic proposals, not mechanical
        diagrams. Compare the free-standing silhouette first, then what remains
        legible under the hull edge and on the chassis surface. Small samples
        remain 1×; no FX or moving parts are shown.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Review decision:</strong>
        the graphic direction is accepted for continued exploration. Carry
        forward 1 Inner ribbon, 2 Swept connection, 4 Inner return and 6
        Terminal sweep. Spring options 7–9 are dropped from this comparison.
        Options 3 and 5 remain reference material, not selected directions.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="depth">
        02.8 / Shading and structure beneath the coil — promising reference
      </summary>
      <p>
        Two new variations of each selected form, one pair per row. A explores
        shallow facets on the existing yellow connection. B adds recessed
        charcoal links beneath the working shape, taking the Potential Well’s
        layered construction as a cue. The aim is a more connected feeling and a
        little more volume while preserving the accepted silhouettes and graphic
        simplicity.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilDepth />
      <p>
        Upper-left light is expressed through a few flat tones. The lower
        structures appear through gaps rather than as backing plates; no offset
        extrusion is used. Compare the enlarged shape and all three 1× views
        before judging whether the added depth earns its visual weight. These
        remain static appearance studies.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Review finding:</strong>
        1A felt whole in the free, underwing and on-chassis speculations. Actual
        game context exposed its dark circular surround as visually separate
        from the ship. It remains a promising construction reference, alongside
        the other retained options; it is not a settled game asset.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="fit">02.9 / Fitting — failed exploration step</summary>
      <p>
        <strong>Failure finding:</strong>
        This step treated a promising drawing as a resolved design. Local
        shading and border corrections displaced the useful question of how a
        complete component belongs with the game’s graphics. The resulting
        revisions created a false sense of progress without resolving that
        question. The bench is retained as an example of an exploration step
        failing; its latest drawing is not an accepted replacement for 1A.
      </p>
      <p>
        One coil on each chassis, with connectors pointing fore–aft and light
        ion engines in every available engine mount. The inner ring follows the
        same upper-left light as the hull. Compare the overlap with the hull and
        the silhouette over space while adjusting size and drawing order. The 1×
        samples retain their actual size.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilFit
        client:load
        wasp={[...WASP.facets]}
        fighterHull={fitFighter}
        mounts={fitMounts}
        engines={fitEngines}
      />
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="belonging">
        02.10 / Whole equipment, part of the ship — findings
      </summary>
      <p>
        A gravity coil needs to feel complete over open space and belong beside
        the ship’s engines. Three construction ideas test where that
        completeness can come from: longitudinal rails, a continuous open body,
        or paired windings sharing a spine. Each keeps a deliberate opening and
        a small number of substantial parts.
      </p>
      <p>
        1A supplies evidence that connected structure helps. Its circular
        surround is not carried forward as a requirement. The real Wasp and
        Fighter are present in every comparison, so isolated appeal cannot stand
        in for visual belonging.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilBelonging
        wasp={[...WASP.facets]}
        fighterHull={fitFighter}
        mounts={fitMounts}
        engines={fitEngines}
      />
      <p>
        <strong>02.10 finding:</strong>
        All three proposals feel acceptable under wing but remain floating icons
        over the chassis, like 1A. Changing the supporting construction did not
        resolve visual belonging. The Potential Well appears to work in both
        configurations. 1A / Inner ribbon remains the reference design.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="seating">
        02.11 / What makes equipment feel seated? — findings
      </summary>
      <p>
        The next comparison keeps 1A and investigates its relationship with the
        surface beneath it. The Potential Well has dark working interiors; the
        coil’s opening reveals the hull. Another possible difference is whether
        the drawing shows the hull holding the equipment. These are separate
        hypotheses, not established causes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The unchanged reference, a contained interior and captured terminals are
        shown beside the production Potential Well. Both ships appear with
        equipment above the hull; Wasp also appears under wing. This separates
        the mounting relationship from the chassis choice.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilSeating mounts={fitMounts} />
      <p>
        <strong>02.11 finding:</strong>
        B reads as a special well or window; C makes the mismatch more
        pronounced. Neither explains the stylistic problem. 1A still looks good
        isolated and over space, but reads as an icon on the chassis.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="planes">02.12 / A symbol or a surface? — findings</summary>
      <p>
        The ship is described by a few broad planes. 1A repeats a small
        dark-edged object eight times around a centre. This comparison tests
        whether that surface organisation creates the icon reading, while
        keeping the ring identity, opening and yellow ribbon.
      </p>
      <p>
        B gives each existing sector one full face. C treats the winding as one
        folded surface, deliberately removing the repeated divisions. The
        unchanged 1A and production Potential Well remain alongside them. No
        seating hardware or filled opening is added.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilPlanes mounts={fitMounts} />
      <p>
        <a href="/studies/gravity-coil/wasp-and-1a-editable.svg" download
          >Download editable Wasp and original 1A SVG · 3× and 1×</a
        >
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>02.12 finding:</strong>
        Removing detail does not make the coil fit the chassis. The simplified
        faces and continuous surface do not resolve the stylistic mismatch. 1A
        remains the reference.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="construction">
        02.13 / Winding, housing and ship — findings
      </summary>
      <p>
        What visual construction makes a gravity coil feel like complete
        equipment on its own and part of this game when fitted to a ship? The 1A
        ring, opening and yellow connections stay. This comparison investigates
        the relationship between working material and supporting structure
        inside that established shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        B tests shared material language with identical geometry. C gives the
        working sectors a dark interior role and the light material a retaining
        role around them. If B helps, material continuity is a useful direction.
        If C helps beyond B, the arrangement of housing and working surfaces is
        worth developing. If neither helps, these constructions can be ruled out
        without reopening the silhouette.
      </p>
      <GravityCoilConstruction mounts={fitMounts} />
      <p>
        <strong>02.13 finding:</strong>
        B and C both improve the fit, but neither resolves it. The intended
        materials are ship and engine colours, with safety-yellow supports under
        starlight. Palette and the way light describes the faces remain possible
        causes.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3 id="light">02.14 / Same equipment, same starlight</h3>
    <p>
      The shape stays. This comparison separates two possibilities: the coil
      uses an unrelated set of tones, or its faces imply lighting that disagrees
      with the ship. The two improved constructions from 02.13 remain as
      controls.
    </p>
    <p>
      B uses production colours with the original face treatment, aligned to
      upper-left light. C uses the same colours but treats the winding as one
      assembly under upper-left light: an outside slope and an inside wall
      cannot both face the light in the same direction. The yellow structure
      follows that light too.
    </p>
    <GravityCoilLight mounts={fitMounts} />
    <p>
      <strong>02.14 finding:</strong>
      C and D are the most promising directions. The plates lost their faceted
      character; restore it while exploring the retaining ring beneath the
      winding.
    </p>
    <h3 id="assembly">02.15 / What holds the winding together?</h3>
    <p>
      The promising C winding stays intact. Three assembly relationships compare
      the exposed construction with a continuous carrier beneath it, then with
      inner and outer supports joined by yellow retaining bridges. The question
      is whether a visible supporting structure makes the coil feel complete and
      fitted, or turns it back into an outlined icon.
    </p>
    <p>
      A keeps C as the reference. B carries the plates on a ring visible around
      the edge and through their gaps. C captures those same plates between
      supports, making the connection explicit. Fighter, engines, mount and
      lighting remain fixed.
    </p>
    <GravityCoilAssembly mounts={fitMounts} />
  </section>
  <section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>
